{"id":133812614623518,"type":"song","name":"Running Scared","artist":"Roy Orbison","year":1961,"weight":12,"info":{"cover":"https://cdn-images.dzcdn.net/images/cover/d7d1c9189bace72bc863e8b2f3c79cfd/250x250-000000-80-0-0.jpg","album":"Running Scared","yt":"E7M-g1fW9XM"},"artist_ref":{"id":244694073052595,"name":"Roy Orbison","info":{"img":"https://cdn-images.dzcdn.net/images/artist/a05bdb2eed3a93309c518e7c89e362d0/1000x1000-000000-80-0-0.jpg"}},"labels":[],"genres":[],"lists":[{"id":132335824845498,"name":"Billboard Year-End Hot 100","src":"charts","grp":"chart","list_year":null,"rank":13,"year":1961,"note":null,"total":6841,"scaruffi":null},{"id":235736466885428,"name":"Billboard Year-End 1961","src":"singles","grp":"billboard_year_end","list_year":1961,"rank":13,"year":1961,"note":"","total":100,"scaruffi":null},{"id":157497903047680,"name":"Billboard Hot 100 nº1 · 1960s","src":"singles","grp":"billboard_number_ones","list_year":1960,"rank":52,"year":1961,"note":"1 semanas","total":226,"scaruffi":null}],"podcast":[{"kind":"podcast","title":null,"body":"Roy Orbison's first number one hit in the US, inspired by Ravel's Boléro. The song featured a significant section in rubato (free time), where musicians could slightly speed up or slow down for expressive effect. This element met resistance from the Nashville A-team, particularly bassist Bob Moore, who preferred a steady beat for pop music. Orbison eventually convinced them to play it as written. This session may have also pioneered a new vocal isolation technique in Nashville, using a coat rack covered with coats to partition the studio and prevent instruments from overpowering Orbison's voice, especially a difficult high 'A' at the song's end. \"I think we should play it the way it was written.\"","year":1961,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"A History of Rock Music in 500 Songs","episode":"Episode 83: \"Only the Lonely\" by Roy Orbison","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=uDvkjXa1ALk"}]}],"book_tracks":[],"other